  "equipment_type": "Fan coil unit controller",
  "applies_to": "Two-pipe fan coil units with one valve for heating or cooling and an optional second heating valve, on the Siemens Two-Pipe Fan Coil BACnet ASC controller 550-433.",
  "summary": "Application 2542 runs a two-pipe fan coil unit on the older Siemens BACnet ASC (terminal equipment) controller over BACnet MS/TP. Valve 1 modulates for cooling or heating depending on the water in the pipe, an optional second valve adds heat in sequence, and the fan runs all day or cycles with valve demand (CYCLE FAN). Heat/cool mode follows the pipe temperature sensor (SUPPLY TEMP against COOL TEMP and HEAT TEMP) or a field panel command.",
  "bacnet_transport": [
    "BACnet MS/TP"
  ],
  "firmware": null,
  "default_device_instance": null,
  "access_inferred": true,
  "notes": [
    "AI and BI objects are read-only. AO and BO objects are BACnet output objects with a priority array; the guide has no access column, so access is inferred from object type. Many AOs are calculated status values, not commands.",
    "Instances shown in braces in the guide may be unbundled at a field panel; that marking is not reproduced here.",
    "Units are given in US units (°F); the guide also lists SI factory defaults.",
    "The APPLICATION point range lists 2528, 2529 and 2542 through 2546 as applications of the same controller; only 2542 is covered here.",
    "Object names are kept as printed, including CLTR ADDRESS, VLVL 2 END, CLT TEMP and CTRL STPT.",
    "If the room or pipe temperature sensor fails, the controller keeps using the last good value."
  ],
  "source": {
    "title": "Two-Pipe Fan Coil BACnet ASC Controller, Application 2542 - Cooling or Heating, Application Notes",
    "doc_number": "140-0749",
    "revision_date": "2015-11",
    "url": "https://sid.siemens.com/api/khub/documents/irhhDwnQMAlC4IiuqChI~g/content"
  },
  "integration": {
    "summary": "Set the MS/TP address, then release the unit to application control. The BMS commands occupancy with DAY.NGT (BO 29) and the day setpoints and, without a pipe sensor, sets heat/cool through SUPPLY TEMP; it reads room temperature, fan status, valve position and the error status.",
    "minimum_points": [
      {
        "ref": "BO 29",
        "name": "DAY.NGT",
        "why": "DAY = occupied, NIGHT = unoccupied"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AO 6",
        "name": "DAY CLG STPT",
        "why": "Occupied cooling setpoint, °F"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AO 7",
        "name": "DAY HTG STPT",
        "why": "Occupied heating setpoint, °F"
      },
      {
        "ref": "BO 5",
        "name": "HEAT.COOL",
        "why": "Current mode: HEAT or COOL"
      },
      {
        "ref": "BO 46",
        "name": "FAN",
        "why": "Fan on/off"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AI 4",
        "name": "ROOM TEMP",
        "why": "Space temperature"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AO 49",
        "name": "VLV 1 POS",
        "why": "Valve 1 position, %"
      },
      {
        "ref": "AO 99",
        "name": "ERROR STATUS",
        "why": "Controller error code"
      }
    ],
    "enable": "Occupancy is DAY.NGT (BO 29). With a wall switch on DI 2 and WALL SWITCH (BO 18) = YES, the switch sets it; standalone with no switch the controller stays in day mode; on a network the field panel or BMS commands DAY.NGT. NGT OVRD (BO 21) shows a temporary night override from the room unit button.",
    "heartbeat": null,
    "remote_sensors": "Without a pipe sensor, the field panel commands SUPPLY TEMP (AI 15) below COOL TEMP for cooling or above HEAT TEMP for heating.",
    "setup": [
      "BACnet MS/TP only. CTLR ADDRESS (AO 1) holds the MS/TP address; the point table lists its factory value as 99.",
      "APPLICATION (AO 2) holds the application number; the point table lists its factory default as 2585."
    ],
    "gotchas": [
      "As shipped, the controller keeps all equipment OFF until it is released to application control (see the Siemens unit conditioner / fan coil start-up procedures).",
      "Loop gains, motor timing, rotation angles and setup codes are commissioning values; don't write them from the BMS routinely."
    ]
  },
